                <pb facs="0638" n="514" sameAs="frus:frus1921v01/pg514"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102 lc/15: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Ruddock</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-08-09T17:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">August 9,
                                1921—5 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received August 9—10:48
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>283. Your 198, July 17 [<hi rendition="#italic">15</hi>], 6 p.m. Is denial of
                    asylum only to follow proscription of offender by the Chinese Government or is
                    the diplomatic body or nationality concerned to decide who is to be <pb
                        facs="0639" n="515" xml:id="pg515"/> denied asylum? For example, Wang,
                    Military Governor of Hupeh, responsible for Ichang lootings appears to be on the
                    point of vacating his post.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Ruddock</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
